Official policy decisions are crucial to a country's development, yet the quality of policymakers' choices can be flawed due to intense political pressures. To address this, we developed a set of guidelines that national governments can implement to improve policymaking: strengthen the rule of law, enhance transparency, set clear long-term goals, and establish a system to monitor and manage public funds effectively. These measures can help mitigate the negative effects of distorted policymaking. Additionally, we suggest strategies for international organizations and non-governmental organizations to support countries in implementing these guidelines, thereby promoting better policy reform policymaking through partnerships and investment in policy networks. Our approach draws on the experiences of Argentina and Brazil, where reforms have been implemented and evaluated over multiple periods. Furthermore, we outline the conditions under which international organizations and non-governmental organizations can collaborate with national authorities to achieve meaningful policy outcomes. Our analysis is grounded in the existing literature on policy reform and the economic benefits of effective public spending. By building on the work of Dreber et al. (2019) and the empirical research of Arndt et al. (2018), we develop a set of practical recommendations for policymakers and international organizations. Finally, we explore the potential risks and limitations of our proposals and offer suggestions for future research that could further refine these guidelines.} 
